        },
        {
            "summary": "Session state includes headers, parameters, and cookies",
            "explanation": "Several tests examine how session state is merged into requests. The evidence covers preservation of ordered headers, omission of headers whose session value is `None`, case-insensitive header removal, case-insensitive header updates, and case-sensitive merging of parameter names. [Data: Entities (1130, 1129, 1268, 1258, 1269); Relationships (1729, 1725, 2060, 2031, 2062)]\n\nCookie behavior is tested separately for persistence across redirects and non-persistence when cookies are supplied only to an individual request. These relationships indicate that the session model distinguishes durable session state from per-request inputs. [Data: Entities (1121, 1123); Relationships (1693, 1695, 1699)]"
        },
        {
            "summary": "Request preparation preserves and transforms client input",
            "explanation": "The community includes `prepare_request` functionality on session objects and tests that prepare requests from session and request data. One test verifies that the original order of parameters in an `OrderedDict` is preserved in the prepared URL, while another tests session preparation when a header value is `None`. [Data: Entities (1094, 1112, 1129, 1109); Relationships (1669, 1728, 1593, 1640)]\n\nThe data also links session headers to updates using differently cased names and records expected case-insensitive behavior. These tests indicate that preparation is a key boundary where session defaults, request-specific values, ordering, and header normalization are combined. [Data: Entities (1259, 1268, 1269); Relationships (2031, 2060, 2062)]"
        },
        {
            "summary": "Redirect authentication handling is a significant capability",
            "explanation": "The `rebuild_auth` routine removes an `Authorization` header when `should_strip_auth` determines that a redirect changes the trust context, then may obtain credentials from trusted environment settings and apply them through request authentication preparation. The data explicitly connects this routine to both authentication rebuilding and the unresolved `should_strip_auth` callable. [Data: Entities (789, 793); Relationships (1056, 1066)]\n\nDedicated tests cover host changes, HTTPS-to-HTTP downgrades, HTTP-to-HTTPS upgrades, port changes, and equivalent default ports. The evidence supports a deliberate redirect policy that treats host changes, downgrades, and certain port changes differently, but it does not establish the full implementation or its behavior outside the listed cases. [Data: Entities (1290, 1291, 1292, 1294, 1295); Relationships (2110, 2111, 2112, 2136, 2138)]"
